# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# StreamPi additions
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Secrets. Every one of these is a live credential; the repo is public.
#   server/.env           Telegram api_id/api_hash/session — see .env.example
#   service-account.json  full Firebase admin access to the project
#   node_config.json      a node's apiKey — see node_config.json.example
#   *.jks / keystore.*    the Android release signing identity
#
# .env / .env.* are already covered by the dotenv block further up, with .env.example
# re-included. server/config.json is listed because it used to be tracked and did leak a
# live Telegram session — keeping the rule means a stray copy can't come back.
server/config.json
server/service-account.json
node/node_config.json
*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties
local.properties
*.key
*.pem
# ...except the local CA certificate, which is a public cert the client ships on purpose.
!web_client/public/streampi-local-ca.pem

# Runtime data, not source: the media library (tens of GB), the SQLite DB holding
# user credentials and live sessions, the published APK, server logs, temp uploads.
server_data/

# Android / Gradle
.gradle/
.kotlin/
.android/
build/
captures/
.externalNativeBuild
.cxx
*.iml

# Editor state
.idea/
.vscode/
